        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Absolute monarchy</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Power held by</span><span class="info-stat-val">The monarch alone, as supreme lawgiver, judge and commander, governing through ministers, councils and officials who serve at the ruler’s pleasure</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Legitimacy from</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hereditary right sanctified by religion: in early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the divine right of kings, by which the ruler answered to God alone; elsewhere such <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How rulers are chosen</span><span class="info-stat-val">By hereditary succession within a dynasty, usually by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>; a monarch left the throne only by death, abdication, deposition or revolution</span></div>
