    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Absolute monarchy placed the making of law, its execution and the final judgement of cases in one hereditary ruler, justified in early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the divine right of kings.</li><li>Rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> built standing armies, bureaucracies and court cultures that laid foundations of the modern state.</li><li>Revolutions between 1688 and 1917 ended absolutism across most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; historians still debate how absolute these monarchs really were, and a few states keep the form today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>’s King’s Law of 1665, the first written constitution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to establish absolute monarchy, declared the king “the most perfect and supreme person on the Earth”, standing “above all human laws”.</p></aside>
    </section>

