    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1576</span><span class="rf-text">Jean Bodin’s Six Books of the Commonwealth defines <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> as absolute and perpetual power</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1661</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> begins his personal rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the death of Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Jules Mazarin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1665</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> signs the King’s Law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first written constitution of absolute monarchy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1688</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> deposes James II and ends moves towards absolute monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1781</span><span class="rf-text">Joseph II grants religious toleration and abolishes personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in the Bohemian lands</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> begins, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> ends absolute monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">Revolutions sweep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> gives up absolutism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> receives a constitution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a> promises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> an elected Duma, ending unlimited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiv.jpg" alt="Louis XIV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_XIV_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyacinthe Rigaud, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In an absolute monarchy the sovereign is the sole source of political authority. Law is the ruler’s will, issued as edicts, ordinances and decrees; ministers, councils and officials hold office at the ruler’s pleasure and answer to the ruler alone; and no parliament, court or estate of the realm can lawfully overrule the crown. The kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 17th and 18th centuries concentrated legislative, executive and judicial power in their own person. The king was the supreme judge of the kingdom and could condemn without appeal, and because all justice flowed from him he could also make and annul laws. Government ran through royal councils and secretaries of state at the centre and, in the provinces, through intendants, royal commissioners who could be recalled at any moment. A standing army paid by the crown, rather than the retinues of great nobles, gave the system its force.</p><p>Absolute did not mean unlimited, even in theory. Jean Bodin, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet, tutor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s heir, held that the king remained bound by divine and natural law and by the fundamental laws of the kingdom, such as the rules of succession, which no king could alter. Bossuet distinguished absolute government, which no subject might lawfully resist, from arbitrary government, which disregarded law and property. In practice rulers needed the cooperation of nobles, clergy, towns and provincial estates to raise taxes and keep order, and in France the parlements, the high courts of law, registered royal edicts and could protest against them. Some absolutisms were written into law: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>’s King’s Law of 1665 declared the king answerable to God alone.</p><p>Absolute monarchy differs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, in which the crown acts within limits set by a constitution, a legislature or binding custom, and government is carried on by ministers, such as a prime minister, who answer to parliament. It also differs from tyranny as classical philosophers defined it: rule in the ruler’s own interest, unrestrained even by moral law. Political scientists distinguish it, too, from personal dictatorship. An absolute monarch rules within established, if often unwritten, rules of hereditary succession and inherited institutions that give the throne its legitimacy, whereas a personalist dictator’s power rests on networks of loyalists and on institutions that have been hollowed out. For this reason hereditary dictatorships, such as the Duvalier regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, are set apart from absolute monarchies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i.jpg" alt="James VI and I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesIEngland_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to John de Critz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rulers who claimed unlimited authority under divine sanction are far older than the idea of absolutism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> declared himself divine in the 21st century BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were regarded as gods on earth, Chinese emperors ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> was revered as the son of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a>. Medieval European kingship was different. Kings were anointed by the Church and claimed to rule by the grace of God, but they shared power with great nobles, with assemblies such as the English Parliament and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a> of the Iberian kingdoms, and with a Church that could excommunicate them and, popes claimed, depose them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> taught that a king must govern according to natural law and respect the customs of his people.</p><p>The absolute monarchies of early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> grew out of the upheavals of the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> armies and new fortifications were expensive, and only states that could tax widely could afford them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>’s claim to judge kings and in Protestant states placed the church under the crown: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-supremacy-1534">Act of Supremacy 1534</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> Supreme Head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. Decades of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">religious civil war</a>, above all the French Wars of Religion of 1562–1598, made order seem the first good of politics. Lawyers drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, whose maxim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeps">princeps</a> legibus solutus est, “the prince is released from the laws”, matched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> absolutus, “released” or “freed”, from which “absolute” comes; the noun absolutism is a much later coinage.</p><p>A line of writers set out the theory. Jean Bodin’s Six Books of the Commonwealth of 1576 defined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> as the absolute and perpetual power of a commonwealth, indivisible and above human law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and from 1603 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, argued in The True Law of Free Monarchies of 1598 that kings answered to God alone, and in 1610 told Parliament that kings were “God’s lieutenants upon earth”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a>’s Leviathan of 1651 grounded unchallengeable sovereignty not in God but in the need to escape a state of nature in which the life of man was “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”. Sir Robert Filmer’s Patriarcha, published in 1680, likened the king’s power to a father’s over his family, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Bossuet preached that kings reign by the will of God, citing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-proverbs">Book of Proverbs</a>: “by me kings reign”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu.jpg" alt="Cardinal Richelieu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champaigne_portrait_richelieu_eb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philippe de Champaigne, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Absolutism reached its classic form in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a>, chief minister of Louis XIII, broke the military power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> and the great nobles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> began his personal rule in 1661, on the death of Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Jules Mazarin</a>, governing without a chief minister. The Estates General did not meet between 1614 and 1789; the court moved to the Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1682; and in 1685 the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> revoked the toleration granted to Protestants by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> made his crown hereditary in 1660, ending the nobles’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigsradet">Council of the Realm</a>, and in 1665 signed the King’s Law; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI of Sweden</a> won near-absolute power from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> in 1680. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a> ruled without Parliament from 1629 to 1640, but civil war, his execution in 1649 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1688 ended moves towards absolute monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>In the 18th century the absolute rulers of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> borrowed the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> (1682–1725) bound the nobility to state service through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-of-ranks">Table of Ranks</a> of 1722, placed the church under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-synod">Holy Synod</a> and built a new bureaucracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> (1740–1786), who called himself the “first servant of the state”, restricted torture and began the codification of Prussian law; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> in her Instruction of 1767; and Joseph II, sole ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands from 1780 to 1790, granted religious toleration and abolished personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in the Bohemian lands in 1781. Historians call this enlightened absolutism, a concept the German historian Wilhelm Roscher first described formally in 1847.</p><p>Outside Europe the Ottoman sultans styled themselves the “Shadow of God on Earth”, Chinese emperors claimed absolute authority under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>, and Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> is likewise counted an absolute monarchy. The age of revolutions undid the form in Europe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> ended absolute monarchy in France in 1789; the revolutions of 1848 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to give up absolutism and Prussia to accept a constitution; and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-manifesto">October Manifesto</a> promised an elected Duma. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> received its first constitution in 1876 and Siam in 1932, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought down the Russian, German, Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman empires. In 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-ii">Sobhuza II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a> suspended the constitution and ruled as an absolute monarch, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>’s kings swung between direct and constitutional rule until the monarchy was abolished in 2008.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes.jpg" alt="Thomas Hobbes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Hobbes_by_John_Michael_Wright_(colour)_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Michael Wright, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The case for absolute monarchy was first a case for order. Writing during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>, Jean Bodin argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> must be undivided, because a commonwealth in which king, nobles and estates shared supreme power would dissolve into faction and civil war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a> drew the same lesson from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>: only a sovereign whom no one could lawfully resist could guarantee the peace on which every other good depended. He judged monarchy the best form of sovereignty because a monarch’s private interest coincided with the public one, his riches, power and honour arising from the wealth and strength of his subjects. The historian J. P. Sommerville stresses that absolutist writers magnified royal power in order to protect the state against anarchy and to answer the theorists of resistance, and the French jurist Jean Domat presented absolute monarchy as the preservation of the natural order God intended.</p><p>Supporters also pointed to what concentrated power could achieve. Absolutist states built standing armies, professional bureaucracies, roads, canals and fortresses, and curbed the private wars and feuds of great nobles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> founded the Academy of Sciences in 1666 and promoted manufactures, trade and a navy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>’s reforms made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> a great European power within a generation. From the 1760s officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the German states gathered trade statistics, harvest reports, death records and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> to plan for long-term economic growth, the programme of the cameralists and the physiocrats.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> gave the argument a reforming edge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>’s court in the early 1750s, believed that an enlightened monarchy was the surest path to progress, and its advocates reasoned that one wise ruler could overcome the resistance of privileged estates, churches and corporations faster than any assembly. Frederick described his principal occupation as combating ignorance and prejudice. Enlightened rulers could point to results: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> restricted judicial torture and in 1763 required elementary schooling, and its school system was later copied abroad; Joseph II freed peasants from personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and granted toleration to Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> (1759–1788) promoted science, trade and agriculture; and in Portugal the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a> directed the rebuilding of the capital after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1755-lisbon-earthquake">1755 Lisbon earthquake</a>. Some historians credit rulers of this kind with a part in the ending of serfdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-locke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-locke.jpg" alt="John Locke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godfrey_Kneller_-_Portrait_of_John_Locke_(Hermitage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godfrey Kneller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Critics answered that a power without limits is a power without remedy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>’s Two Treatises of Government (1689) declared that absolute monarchy “is indeed inconsistent with civil society, and so can be no form of civil-government at all”, since subjects had no common judge to appeal to between themselves and their ruler. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>’s The Spirit of the Laws (1748) distinguished monarchy, governed by fixed laws through intermediate powers such as the nobility and the parlements, from despotism, in which one man rules by his own will and caprice, and warned that a monarchy which destroyed those intermediate powers would become a despotism. The Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Juan de Mariana argued in De rege et regis institutione (1598) that a people which had made a king could call him to account and, in extreme cases, kill a tyrant.</p><p>The record supplied examples. Concentrated power could be turned against subjects: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a> unleashed a reign of terror through the oprichnina from 1565, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s revocation of the Edict of Nantes drove an estimated 200,000 to 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">French Protestants</a> into exile. French kings could imprison subjects without trial by lettres de cachet. Everything depended on the person of the ruler. Minorities, incapable heirs and ambitious favourites were constant dangers, and reforms imposed from above were easily reversed. Joseph II announced so many unwelcome reforms that revolts broke out in the Austrian Netherlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> relapsed after the death of Charles III; and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-friedrich-struensee">Johann Friedrich Struensee</a>, the royal physician who governed for the mentally ill Christian VII and issued more than a thousand decrees in little more than a year, was overthrown and executed in 1772.</p><p>Finance exposed the gap between claim and reality. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> historian William Bouwsma observed that nothing showed the limits of royal power so clearly as the fact that governments were perennially in financial trouble, unable to tap the wealth of those best able to pay and likely to provoke a costly revolt whenever they tried to raise an adequate income. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the nobility and clergy largely escaped direct taxation, and debts from a century of wars, ending with the American War of Independence, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> to summon the Estates General, which met in May 1789; within weeks its commoners had proclaimed themselves a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> and the absolute monarchy was at an end. Absolute rule offered no regular way of winning consent to new taxes or reforms, and in France in 1789, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1905 and 1917, financial or military failure turned into revolution.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville.jpg" alt="Alexis de Tocqueville" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville">Alexis de Tocqueville</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Alexis_de_Tocqueville.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Théodore Chassériau, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Absolute monarchy left the modern state much of its machinery: centralised administration, professional bureaucracies, standing armies, codified law and the idea of a single sovereign authority within fixed borders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexis-de-tocqueville">Alexis de Tocqueville</a> argued in The Old Regime and the Revolution (1856) that the centralised administration of the French monarchy survived the Revolution and passed to the governments that followed it. The struggle against absolutism produced its opposites: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Locke’s theory of government by consent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>’s separation of powers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence</a>, which accused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> of seeking “an absolute Tyranny over these States”.</p><p>A handful of states are still governed this way. Most surveys list Brunei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, Qatar and Saudi Arabia as absolute monarchies. In Saudi Arabia the Basic Law of 1992 declares the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the Sunnah to be the constitution, and political parties and national elections are not permitted; in Oman the sultan is both head of state and head of government. The pope rules Vatican City as an elected absolute monarch. Several other monarchies, among them Jordan, Kuwait and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, have adopted constitutions while their monarchs keep wide powers, and in Liechtenstein a referendum in 2003 enlarged the prince’s powers to dismiss the government and to veto legislation.</p><p>Historians now debate how absolute the absolute monarchs ever were. Perry Anderson’s Lineages of the Absolutist State (1974) interpreted absolutism as “a redeployed and recharged apparatus of feudal domination”, serving the landed nobility in an age of growing trade. Studies such as William Beik’s Absolutism and Society in Seventeenth-Century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (1985), on the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>, and Roger Mettam’s Power and Faction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s France (1988) found that the crown governed by bargaining with provincial elites, rewarding their cooperation rather than overriding them, and Nicholas Henshall questioned the whole concept in The Myth of Absolutism (1992). Most scholars still use the term for the great monarchies of the 17th and 18th centuries, while stressing the distance between the rhetoric of unlimited power and the means of rulers who had to negotiate with nobles, churches, towns and creditors.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Absolute monarchy concentrated all power in a hereditary ruler answerable, in theory, to God alone. Justified by Bodin, Hobbes and the doctrine of divine right, it built the armies, bureaucracies and courts of early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, before revolutions between 1688 and 1917 replaced it with constitutional government almost everywhere.</em></p>

